/E (is far more dangerous than raising a false alarm. For example, if a patient develops fever, low blood pressure, high respiratory rate, and low oxygen saturation, but the model predicts that the patient is stable, the clinical team may not intervene quickly. This false negative could delay antibiotics, fluids, ICU transfer, or closer monitoring. A false positive may create extra workload, but a false negative may cost a patient's life. Therefore, the model evaluation focused strongly )
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [10]
>>
