54 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 38 0 R
/T (Section 2: Baseline Design Decisions  The first generation model used a shallow deep neural network as a baseline. This model  treated each hourly clinical record as an independent snapshot. Missing values were handled using median imputation, features were normalized using standard scaling, and columns with more than 95 percent missing values were removed. Class weighting was used because only a small portion of the dataset represented sepsis or deterioration cases. )
/E (Section 2: Baseline Design Decisions  The first generation model used a shallow deep neural network as a baseline. This model  treated each hourly clinical record as an independent snapshot. Missing values were handled using median imputation, features were normalized using standard scaling, and columns with more than 95 percent missing values were removed. Class weighting was used because only a small portion of the dataset represented sepsis or deterioration cases. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
55 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 38 0 R
/T (One important issue in deep learning is the vanishing gradient problem. In deep networks, gradients can become very small as they are propagated backward through many layers. When this happens, early layers learn very slowly, and the network may fail to capture useful patterns. ReLU activation was used because it reduces this problem compared to sigmoid or tanh activations. Batch Normalization was also added because it stabilizes the distribution of layer inputs, supports faster convergence, and helps the model train more reliably. )
/E (One important issue in deep learning is the vanishing gradient problem. In deep networks, gradients can become very small as they are propagated backward through many layers. When this happens, early layers learn very slowly, and the network may fail to capture useful patterns. ReLU activation was used because it reduces this problem compared to sigmoid or tanh activations. Batch Normalization was also added because it stabilizes the distribution of layer inputs, supports faster convergence, and helps the model train more reliably. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
56 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 38 0 R
/T (The DNN was trained using two optimizers: Adam and SGD. Adam achieved better overall accuracy and F1-score, while SGD trained faster and produced slightly higher recall. Adam adapts the learning rate for each parameter and usually converges more smoothly, while SGD with momentum is simpler and computationally lighter but may require more careful tuning. In  this experiment, Adam was selected as the final DNN baseline because it gave a stronger  overall balance of metrics. )
/E (The DNN was trained using two optimizers: Adam and SGD. Adam achieved better overall accuracy and F1-score, while SGD trained faster and produced slightly higher recall. Adam adapts the learning rate for each parameter and usually converges more smoothly, while SGD with momentum is simpler and computationally lighter but may require more careful tuning. In  this experiment, Adam was selected as the final DNN baseline because it gave a stronger  overall balance of metrics. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
57 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 38 0 R
/T (A loss function measures the error for one prediction or one batch during training, while a cost function usually refers to the overall average objective minimized across the dataset. Binary cross-entropy was used because this is a binary classification problem: stable patient versus deterioration risk. Dropout was added as a regularization technique. It randomly disables some neurons during training, which prevents the network from relying too heavily on specific features and reduces overfitting. )
/E (A loss function measures the error for one prediction or one batch during training, while a cost function usually refers to the overall average objective minimized across the dataset. Binary cross-entropy was used because this is a binary classification problem: stable patient versus deterioration risk. Dropout was added as a regularization technique. It randomly disables some neurons during training, which prevents the network from relying too heavily on specific features and reduces overfitting. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
58 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 39 0 R
/T (Section 3: Modelling Patient Timelines  The second generation models used recurrent architectures to capture patient timelines. Instead of treating each row as a separate snapshot, the data was restructured into 12-hour patient windows. This is more clinically realistic because deterioration is often a story that unfolds  across time. A patient's heart rate, oxygen saturation, temperature, blood pressure, and respiratory rate may slowly worsen before a severe event occurs. )
/E (Section 3: Modelling Patient Timelines  The second generation models used recurrent architectures to capture patient timelines. Instead of treating each row as a separate snapshot, the data was restructured into 12-hour patient windows. This is more clinically realistic because deterioration is often a story that unfolds  across time. A patient's heart rate, oxygen saturation, temperature, blood pressure, and respiratory rate may slowly worsen before a severe event occurs. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
59 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 39 0 R
/T (RNNs can suffer from the vanishing gradient problem even more severely than standard feedforward networks because information must be passed across many time steps. If gradients shrink over time, the model may forget earlier clinical signals that are still important. LSTM networks address this issue using gates: the input gate controls what new information is stored, the forget gate controls what information is removed, and the output gate controls what information is passed forward. This allows LSTMs to preserve important signals over longer sequences. )
/E (RNNs can suffer from the vanishing gradient problem even more severely than standard feedforward networks because information must be passed across many time steps. If gradients shrink over time, the model may forget earlier clinical signals that are still important. LSTM networks address this issue using gates: the input gate controls what new information is stored, the forget gate controls what information is removed, and the output gate controls what information is passed forward. This allows LSTMs to preserve important signals over longer sequences. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
60 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 39 0 R
/T (GRU models simplify the LSTM design by combining some gate mechanisms. This makes  GRUs faster and lighter, which can be useful in real-time clinical monitoring where latency and computational cost matter. However, in this experiment, GRU produced many false positives  and a lower F1-score than LSTM. The LSTM achieved the best balance, with accuracy of  0.9000, recall of 0.5714, and F1-score of 0.5000. )
/E (GRU models simplify the LSTM design by combining some gate mechanisms. This makes  GRUs faster and lighter, which can be useful in real-time clinical monitoring where latency and computational cost matter. However, in this experiment, GRU produced many false positives  and a lower F1-score than LSTM. The LSTM achieved the best balance, with accuracy of  0.9000, recall of 0.5714, and F1-score of 0.5000. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
61 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 39 0 R
/T (The Bi-LSTM did not improve performance in this experiment. More importantly, bidirectional LSTM is not appropriate for a live ICU early warning system because it uses both past and  future context. In real-time monitoring, future measurements are not available at the moment of prediction. Bi-LSTM may be useful for retrospective analysis, research, or offline chart review,  but a unidirectional LSTM is more appropriate for deployment because it only uses information available up to the current time. )
/E (The Bi-LSTM did not improve performance in this experiment. More importantly, bidirectional LSTM is not appropriate for a live ICU early warning system because it uses both past and  future context. In real-time monitoring, future measurements are not available at the moment of prediction. Bi-LSTM may be useful for retrospective analysis, research, or offline chart review,  but a unidirectional LSTM is more appropriate for deployment because it only uses information available up to the current time. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
62 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 40 0 R
/T (Section 4: The Transformer and Clinical Language  Pre-trained language models are useful because they bring knowledge learned from large text corpora into a smaller clinical task. In the context of ClinicalBERT, transfer learning means that the model has already learned medical vocabulary, clinical phrasing, abbreviation patterns, and relationships between healthcare terms from large biomedical or clinical text sources. Training a language model from scratch on a small assignment dataset would not provide enough data for meaningful language understanding. )
/E (Section 4: The Transformer and Clinical Language  Pre-trained language models are useful because they bring knowledge learned from large text corpora into a smaller clinical task. In the context of ClinicalBERT, transfer learning means that the model has already learned medical vocabulary, clinical phrasing, abbreviation patterns, and relationships between healthcare terms from large biomedical or clinical text sources. Training a language model from scratch on a small assignment dataset would not provide enough data for meaningful language understanding. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
63 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 40 0 R
/T (Self-attention is valuable because clinical notes are not always read in a simple left-to-right manner. A doctor may scan for key warning terms such as fever, hypotension, tachypnea,  altered mental status, infection, or oxygen desaturation. Transformer models use self-attention  to connect important words across the note and identify which terms contribute most to the classification decision. )
/E (Self-attention is valuable because clinical notes are not always read in a simple left-to-right manner. A doctor may scan for key warning terms such as fever, hypotension, tachypnea,  altered mental status, infection, or oxygen desaturation. Transformer models use self-attention  to connect important words across the note and identify which terms contribute most to the classification decision. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
64 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 40 0 R
/T (Since the PhysioNet Sepsis dataset does not include real free-text notes, synthetic notes were generated from vital-sign abnormalities. The simulated attention-style visualization highlighted terms such as fever, hypotension, tachypnea, and tachycardia. These are clinically meaningful warning signs and align with what a clinician would expect in sepsis or deterioration risk. This suggests that the text model focused on medically relevant terms rather than unrelated words. )
/E (Since the PhysioNet Sepsis dataset does not include real free-text notes, synthetic notes were generated from vital-sign abnormalities. The simulated attention-style visualization highlighted terms such as fever, hypotension, tachypnea, and tachycardia. These are clinically meaningful warning signs and align with what a clinician would expect in sepsis or deterioration risk. This suggests that the text model focused on medically relevant terms rather than unrelated words. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
