<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 40 0 R
/T (Two fine-tuning strategies were compared. The frozen simulation represented using a  pre-trained model as a fixed feature extractor with only the classification head trained. This approach is faster, cheaper, and safer when the dataset is small. The full fine-tuning simulation represented updating the full model. It achieved slightly better accuracy, precision, and  F1-score, but in a real Transformer this would require more GPU memory, more training time,  and greater risk of overfitting. Frozen fine-tuning is preferable when the dataset is small or computational resources are limited. Full fine-tuning is justified when there is enough labeled clinical text and the deployment benefit outweighs the training cost. )
/E (Two fine-tuning strategies were compared. The frozen simulation represented using a  pre-trained model as a fixed feature extractor with only the classification head trained. This approach is faster, cheaper, and safer when the dataset is small. The full fine-tuning simulation represented updating the full model. It achieved slightly better accuracy, precision, and  F1-score, but in a real Transformer this would require more GPU memory, more training time,  and greater risk of overfitting. Frozen fine-tuning is preferable when the dataset is small or computational resources are limited. Full fine-tuning is justified when there is enough labeled clinical text and the deployment benefit outweighs the training cost. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
