/E (Based on the experimental results, the LSTM model is the recommended deployment candidate for a real ICU early warning system. It achieved the best overall balance between accuracy, )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
68 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 41 0 R
/E (recall, F1-score, and real-time usability. The DNN baseline was simpler but ignored patient timeline patterns. The GRU was faster but produced too many false positives. The Bi-LSTM was not suitable for live monitoring because it depends on future context. The simulated )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
69 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 41 0 R
/E (ClinicalBERT models were useful for explanation and note analysis, but the text component was synthetic because the selected dataset did not include real clinical notes. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
70 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 41 0 R
/E (In a real deployment, the safest architecture would combine a unidirectional LSTM for real-time vital-sign monitoring with a ClinicalBERT-style note model once real clinical notes are available. This would allow the system to use both structured physiological trends and unstructured )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
71 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 41 0 R
/E (clinician observations. The final output should not replace doctors. It should act as a decision-support system that highlights high-risk patients and explains the warning signals. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
72 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 41 0 R
/E (Several ethical concerns must be addressed before clinical deployment. Bias is a major risk because models trained on one hospital population may not perform equally well across age groups, genders, ethnic groups, or care settings. Privacy is also critical because medical data is highly sensitive and must be protected through secure storage, access control, and de-identification where appropriate. Accountability must also be clear. If the model misses a deteriorating patient or raises repeated false alarms, the hospital must define who is responsible for reviewing and acting on the alert. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
73 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 41 0 R
/E (If the hospital later wanted to analyze chest X-rays alongside clinical notes and vitals, the architecture would need to become multimodal. A CNN or Vision Transformer could process )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
74 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 41 0 R
/E (chest X-ray images, ClinicalBERT could process notes, and LSTM could process time-series vitals. The outputs from all three branches could be fused into a final classification layer that predicts deterioration risk. This would create a richer early warning system, but it would also require stronger validation, explainability, and clinical governance before deployment. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
75 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 41 0 R
