/Title (Can AI Save Lives? Building an Intelligent Clinical Early Warning System)
/Dest [9 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 103 0 R
>>
endobj
126 0 obj
<<
/Title (Section 2: Baseline Design Decisions  The first generation model used a shallow deep neural network as a baseline. This model  treated each hourly clinical record as an independent snapshot. Missing values were handled using median imputation, features were normalized using standard scaling, and columns with more than 95 percent missing values were removed. Class weighting was used because only a small portion of the dataset represented sepsis or deterioration cases.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 103 0 R
/Next 148 0 R
>>
endobj
127 0 obj
<<
/Title (Since the PhysioNet Sepsis dataset does not include real free-text notes, synthetic notes were generated from vital-sign abnormalities. The simulated attention-style visualization highlighted terms such as fever, hypotension, tachypnea, and tachycardia. These are clinically meaningful warning signs and align with what a clinician would expect in sepsis or deterioration risk. This suggests that the text model focused on medically relevant terms rather than unrelated words.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 149 0 R
/Next 104 0 R
>>
endobj
